---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="About — Chris Schmidt"
  description="About Chris Schmidt, ML Engineer at HII Mission Technologies and JHU AI Engineering MS student."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h1 class="section-title">About</h1>

      <div class="about-content">
        <p>
          I studied applied mathematics at Towson University (MS, 2021) and have been working in
          data and software engineering ever since. The math background shapes how I approach ML
          problems: I want to understand the mechanism, not just the API surface. That means reading
          the papers, implementing from scratch when it clarifies something, and measuring results
          with proper eval harnesses rather than eyeballing outputs.
        </p>

        <p>
          I have spent five years at HII Mission Technologies. I started in 2021 as a data engineer
          on the Fortis AI program, building serverless ingestion pipelines on AWS and integrating
          BERT-based NLP models. From 2022 to 2024, as a software engineer, I led data analysis and
          quality work for DoD data modernization on the Advana platform, alongside pipeline and
          exploratory tooling work (OCR, translation, geospatial, and graph analysis) for Fortis AI.
          Since October 2024 I have been an ML Engineer supporting the F-35 Joint Program Office's
          data modernization initiative, building Python analysis tools and dashboards on AWS
          GovCloud. I hold an active security clearance.
        </p>

        <p>
          Before moving into engineering, I spent more than two decades in sales and client-facing
          roles: geotechnical engineering product sales, financial advising at Morgan Stanley,
          institutional fixed income sales at Wachovia Securities, small business banking, mortgage
          lending, and commercial telecom sales at MCI. That background shapes how I work as an
          engineer. I listen for what stakeholders actually need, translate technical work into
          business terms, and build relationships that last beyond a single project.
        </p>

        <p>
          In 2025 I enrolled in the MS in AI Engineering program at Johns Hopkins University, taking
          one course a semester (expected May 2029). The program is a deliberate deepening: inference
          optimization, LLM internals, evaluation methodology, and the mathematical foundations that
          make those things legible. Several projects on this site started from that coursework;
          SiteWatch AI, for example, grew out of the computer vision component of Creating
          AI-Enabled Systems.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .about-content {
    max-width: 680px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .about-content p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: #374151;
  }
</style>
